--TEST--
A spawned child's own `return` is the value the parent reads off the awaited result, one handle at a time and in whatever order the parent awaits them — ADR 0006 § *Failure is a value, not an exception*, ADR 0072 § 4
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`: `spawn script` goes through a door that asks for
# `script.spawn` first, and deny-by-default means a case with no
# configuration cannot spawn at all. This grants it and nothing else.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true
--FILE adder.nvs--
<?nvs
// The child's whole job is to answer a value. A file-scope `return` is what
// crosses (`rule:statements/a-require-expression-is-mixed`'s value form, read here through the isolate boundary),
// and it crosses as `rule:classes/graph-copy`'s graph copy — so a structured answer arrives
// whole rather than as a handle onto the child's arena, which no longer exists
// by the time the parent reads it.
var $total = 0;
foreach (Core\Arr::range(1, 10) as int $n) {
    $total += $n;
}

return ["sum" => $total, "count" => 10];
--FILE namer.nvs--
<?nvs
// A second child, so the awaits below are two distinct answers rather than one
// answer read twice.
return "namer";
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`'s result shape has four fields and this case is the one no other
// isolate case reads: `value`. `ok`, `output` and `error` are pinned by
// tests/conformance/isolate/, and what is left is the ordinary success path —
// a child that ran, returned, and handed its answer back.

// Both children are spawned before either is awaited, which is what makes the
// await a *join* rather than a call: the handle outlives the statement that
// produced it and the value is collected where the parent asks for it.
var $sum = spawn script "adder.nvs";
var $name = spawn script "namer.nvs";

// The parent's own work, in between. A spawn that were really a synchronous
// call would still print this line — what it could not do is print it before
// either child had been joined, which is the ordering below.
echo "parent working", "\n";

// Awaited in the opposite order to the spawns, so a result queue that answered
// in completion order rather than per handle fails here.
var $named = await $name;
echo "value=", $named->value as string, "\n";

var $added = await $sum;
echo "ok=", $added->ok ? "true" : "false", "\n";

// `value` is `mixed` — `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing` cannot know a child's return type statically —
// so reading a structured answer costs the cast that every `mixed` read costs
// (`rule:types/grammar`). 1+2+…+10 = 55.
var $answer = $added->value as array<int>;
echo "sum=", $answer["sum"], "\n";
echo "count=", $answer["count"], "\n";
--EXPECT--
parent working
value=namer
ok=true
sum=55
count=10
